# 13. OBJECT MANAGEMENT — VISIBILITY, HIDING, AND ACTUAL REMOVAL

This requirement is particularly important.

The user specifically wants the ability to select document elements, hide them, restore their visibility, and remove them.

The application must provide a real object-management experience rather than a visual-only overlay trick.

## 13.1 Object types

Where technically identifiable and safely manipulable, support:

- Text objects.
- Text blocks.
- Images.
- Supported vector graphics.
- Supported shapes.
- Annotations.
- Editor-managed elements.
- Other identifiable PDF elements where safe.

Do not assume every visual item on a PDF page is an independent object.

PDF content can be represented by complex content streams, grouped text runs, clipping paths, embedded resources, transparency groups, Form XObjects, annotations, and other structures.

Object boundaries may not correspond to the visible elements a user expects.

## 13.2 Object and layer panel

Implement a dedicated object-management panel.

For supported elements, display:

- Object type.
- Object name or generated label.
- Page association.
- Visibility state.
- Lock state.
- Selection state.
- Stacking position.

The panel should support:

- Select.
- Rename editor-managed elements.
- Hide.
- Show.
- Lock.
- Unlock.
- Delete.
- Reorder.
- Multiselect.
- Contextual operations.

Use compact, clear rows with visible state indicators.

Do not overload the list with unnecessary details.

## 13.3 Hide and restore

The user must be able to hide supported objects and restore them later.

Provide:

- Visibility toggle.
- Clear hidden-state indication.
- Ability to restore hidden objects.
- Multiple-object selection.
- Show all and hide selected where appropriate.
- Undo and redo support.

Make it clear whether hiding is:

1. An editor-only visibility change.
2. A change that will be applied to the exported PDF.

Do not imply that an editor-only hide state has permanently modified the document.

If exported visibility changes are implemented, verify the actual output.

## 13.4 Actual object removal

Allow deletion of supported objects, including:

- Selected text objects.
- Supported text blocks.
- Images.
- Supported shapes.
- Annotations.
- Editor-managed elements.

Provide:

- Delete action.
- Context menu.
- Keyboard shortcut.
- Confirmation where appropriate.
- Undo.
- Clear selection feedback.
- Export and output verification.

**Deleting an object must remove the underlying content from the exported PDF where the operation claims to be a true removal.**

A white rectangle placed over an object is not object removal.

If an element cannot safely be isolated, do not delete unrelated content. Explain the limitation.

## 13.5 Object context menu

Where supported, provide contextual actions:

- Hide.
- Show.
- Delete.
- Duplicate.
- Lock.
- Unlock.
- Bring forward.
- Send backward.
- Bring to front.
- Send to back.
- Align.
- Resize.
- Rotate.
- Inspect properties.

Only expose actions that genuinely work for the selected object type.

## 13.6 Layer management

Support a structured layer and object-management experience.

Where appropriate:

- Group and ungroup supported editor-managed elements.
- Reorder elements.
- Rename editor-managed groups.
- Control visibility.
- Lock and unlock.
- Select multiple objects.
- Manage stacking order.

Distinguish native PDF optional-content layers from editor-managed object collections.

Do not claim that every PDF contains native layers.

## 13.7 Object-management acceptance criteria

For each supported object type, verify the actual workflow:

1. Select the object.
2. Hide the object.
3. Verify that it is hidden in the editor.
4. Restore its visibility.
5. Verify that it reappears.
6. Delete it.
7. Verify the deletion in the editor.
8. Undo the deletion.
9. Verify restoration.
10. Export the document.
11. Reopen or render the exported document.
12. Verify that the final output reflects the intended change.

Test multiselection and stacking order where implemented.

