# 22. APPLICATION SECURITY AND PRIVACY

Treat uploaded documents as untrusted input.

Security applies to guests and registered users equally.

## 22.1 File handling

- Validate file signatures.
- Validate actual formats.
- Restrict unsafe file types.
- Use safe filename handling.
- Prevent path traversal.
- Limit file size and page count appropriately.
- Reject malformed input safely.
- Avoid trusting client-provided metadata.
- Isolate temporary processing files.

## 22.2 URL import

If URL import is implemented:

- Prevent SSRF.
- Block access to internal and restricted network destinations.
- Validate redirects.
- Restrict protocols.
- Enforce download limits.
- Apply timeouts.
- Validate downloaded content.

## 22.3 Processing isolation

- Isolate jobs.
- Apply CPU and memory limits.
- Apply execution timeouts.
- Handle worker crashes.
- Prevent one job from affecting another.
- Avoid public, guessable document URLs.
- Clean up temporary files according to the actual retention policy.

## 22.4 Application security

Implement and test appropriate controls for:

- Authentication.
- Authorization.
- Session management.
- CSRF.
- XSS.
- Injection.
- SSRF.
- Insecure direct object references.
- Cross-user document access.
- Path traversal.
- Resource exhaustion.
- Rate limiting.
- Sensitive information in logs.
- Unsafe error responses.

## 22.5 Privacy

- Guest documents should use temporary processing.
- Do not retain guest documents persistently by default.
- Avoid unnecessary storage of document previews and sensitive content.
- Provide explicit retention and deletion controls for persistent account storage.
- Clearly communicate actual storage and retention behaviour.
- Avoid misleading privacy claims.

Do not claim automatic deletion, end-to-end encryption, zero-knowledge architecture, or regulatory compliance unless it is genuinely implemented and verified.

